one of the sort of most pressing and  important questions for the political  left and I guess really for Democrats to  to the extent that Democrats are a  possible representation of the actual  left instead of corporate interests  which is probably a debate we can have  in and of itself the question is what  kinds of candidates running as Democrats  would do well in the 2018 midterm and  even more generally this is a question  right and this gets asked of me often  during live calls we hear about it and  voicemails I get emails about it and the  question is usually framed as hey David  do you think Democrats are better off  running to the middle are they better  off running really progressive  candidates what is it that Democrats  should do and the answer is as is often  the case in the real world that there  isn't one answer the correct candidate  to take over a district held by a  corporate dem is going to be a different  candidate than the right candidate to  defeat a Republican incumbent for  example in a very red district versus a  blue district versus a swing district so  today I want to focus on swing districts  and there's actually a new study done by  Democratic pollster Celinda Lake and it  looked at 30 swing districts so we're  already narrowing the focus from how  should Democrats run in all elections to  how should they run in swing districts  and then from there the study only  looked specifically at 30 swing swing  districts and the idea was figure out in  those 30 swing districts what type of  Democratic candidate what types of ideas  would do best in an election and it's  very very common that pundits and  commentators will look at a swing  district they'll see roughly the same  percentage of registered Democrats and  Republicans and so they'll come away  saying listen that the best shot a  Democrat has is as a moderate Democrat  in order to win because you've got about  the same number of Democrats and  Republicans you're going to need to  appeal to some of both what the polling  done by Celinda Lake suggests is very  different there the polling that celinda  Lake has released suggests that  Democrats would do better in these swing  districts if they  to the left and the argument there is  that when you pull the voters in these  swing districts on issues and again that  doesn't mean just Republicans and  Democrats that also includes  independents they on average are to the  left of the average corporate Democrat  and certainly to the left of the average  Republican candidates so as an example  in these thirty swing districts the  voters polled by a seventy five percent  with a proportion of seventy five to 25  supported Medicare for all seventy five  percent support Medicare for all in a  swing district that would suggest that a  candidate who supports Medicare for all  would do better in that district  cracking down on Wall Street as another  example protecting Social Security and  Medicare there are lots of policies that  polled very well more than fifty percent  at minimum so why would a Democrat run  to the middle right the numbers seem to  very clearly suggest that actually  Democrats will be better off running  real progressives in these purple or  swing districts and more interesting is  that most of those thirty districts  where the polling took place currently  are held by Republican incumbents and  even still even though the voters in  recent elections voted for Republicans  these leftist policies had at minimum  fifty two percent support people saying  I'd be fifty two percent saying they'd  be more likely to support a candidate  with the progressive ideas only twenty  six percent saying they'd be less likely  to support that candidate so this seems  pretty clear cut and dry but there are  some caveats one caveat is this only  looked at 30 districts I've mentioned  that a couple times because I do think  it's important this strategy would not  necessarily work in every district and  that's why I think it's important not to  think about one strategy as these  strategy the other issue is that the  sample size was 600 to 900 generally  speaking if it's sampled correctly it's  a statistically significant sample but  you don't necessarily want to go around  making national strategy on the basis of  600 to 900 people in just 30 districts  but the main point is that trying to  thread the needle and being 1% to the  left of the incumbent be it a  Kratts or a Republican in a lot of  places it's a losing strategy especially  when generally speaking the candidate  who raises more money wins so if you've  got a corporate democrat or republican  incumbent and then a challenger that's  marginally to their left but can't raise  as much money more than likely to  candidate with more money is going to  win if you can at least separate from  just who can run more ads based on  fundraising and say hey here we have a  candidate that is actually more to the  left whose ideas actually seem to appeal  to more than 50% of the voters that  might even be a way to sort of dig into  the overwhelming advantage that the  candidate that has more money ultimately  has so very very interesting data not  necessarily something to develop a  national strategy around them no it's  interesting it seems like the third rail  for Democrats and these swing districts  it's it's not medicare-for-all or it's  not free college it tends to be abortion  it tends to be gun rights and those are  issues that even more centrist Democrats  are susceptible to losing voters over  yeah I'm not so sure I don't think you  know when we've talked to Bill Scherrer  he seems to think that the gun issue is  just not a voting issue and that it's  not really one that that's making a  difference I mean maybe maybe it's  helping to raise money or affecting  campaigns but I've not I've not seen  evidence of that that that guns is  really sort of a third rail issue in a  lot of these but it's interesting let's  see what the au